00:19:27.880 --> 00:19:31.240
weren't afraid of anybody. Well, a little psychopathic

00:19:31.240 --> 00:19:34.280
to me. That's kind of stupid to me because those

00:19:34.280 --> 00:19:36.920
bullets will take you right out. I'd have a, I would

00:19:36.920 --> 00:19:39.160
have a plate right up there. You ain't gonna get

00:19:39.240 --> 00:19:41.480
my nuts out of having another plate around them.

00:19:41.480 --> 00:19:46.360
Okay, so now you're zeroing in on Abul, Zakataka, whatever

00:19:46.360 --> 00:19:48.760
his name is, and what happens? How do you catch him?

00:19:49.720 --> 00:19:53.480
He made a mistake, Beau. He made a stupid mistake and we

00:19:53.480 --> 00:19:57.320
had counted on him making a mistake. He accessed

00:19:57.320 --> 00:19:59.800
his hotmail account with a landline.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:05.840
And as soon as he did it, I mean, within five minutes, we don't tell too much technology

00:20:05.840 --> 00:20:10.320
because there might be some abadabadou's listening. We have great technology, believe it or not.

00:20:11.440 --> 00:20:16.800
This is 18 year old information, so it's all different now. But sure enough, that address was

00:20:16.800 --> 00:20:25.680
one of the 14 on our list. And that night at 0200, we busted down 14 doors simultaneously as the

00:20:25.680 --> 00:20:30.000
clock struck, struck two and... You have to kill anybody?

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:35.360
Grabbed everybody. No, my orders very specifically were to take them alive. But this

00:20:35.360 --> 00:20:41.920
idiot Pakistani cop shot him three times with an AK-47, shot him in the thigh, the groin in the

00:20:41.920 --> 00:20:45.920
stomach. Oh, he got one of his bullpigs, so they can't be no more of them. That's good.

00:20:45.920 --> 00:20:49.040
Oh, it was a good shot. It was a bloody mess, let me tell you.

00:20:49.840 --> 00:20:58.640
And so, a bunch of us threw him into the back of a filthy Toyota pickup truck.

00:20:58.640 --> 00:21:03.440
We took him to Faisalabad Hospital, middle of the night, 3.30 in the morning.

00:21:04.480 --> 00:21:09.440
We shocked the doctor. All these Americans walking in at 3.30 in the morning dressed

00:21:09.440 --> 00:21:14.960
as Pakistanis with an Arab who's bleeding to death. And I told the doctor, listen,

00:21:15.040 --> 00:21:19.120
my orders were to take him alive. You need to sew him up. And I mean right now.

00:21:19.680 --> 00:21:21.520
So they took him into the operating room. They're trying to sew him up.

00:21:21.520 --> 00:21:23.280
But you had Pakistani guys with you.

00:21:24.400 --> 00:21:25.920
Yeah, we had Pakistani guys with us.

00:21:25.920 --> 00:21:26.720
The squat guys.

00:21:27.520 --> 00:21:31.760
Yeah, but there were a bunch of blonde-haired, blue-eyed CIA guys wearing shower camis that

00:21:31.760 --> 00:21:33.200
looked ridiculous.

00:21:33.200 --> 00:21:35.680
All right, so now you sew the guy up. Where do you bring him then?

00:21:36.400 --> 00:21:40.000
Well, the problem that we had is while he was in surgery,

00:21:40.000 --> 00:21:43.520
word got around the al-Qaeda community that we had gotten him and they started

00:21:43.520 --> 00:21:46.720
driving past the hospital and just opening fire on the hospital.

00:21:46.720 --> 00:21:47.040
Wow.

00:21:47.040 --> 00:21:50.320
So I told this Pakistani major that I was with. I said,

00:21:50.320 --> 00:21:53.680
look, if they realized we're so lightly armed, we're dead.

00:21:53.680 --> 00:21:55.920
Can you get a helicopter in here? He said, yeah.

00:21:56.480 --> 00:22:00.000
20 minutes later, a helicopter lands in the parking lot.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:03.440
I walk right into the operating room, kind of like this.

00:22:03.440 --> 00:22:05.760
I told the doctor, wrap it up. We got to go.

00:22:06.560 --> 00:22:08.400
They sewed him up as fast as they could.

00:22:08.400 --> 00:22:10.080
We wheeled him onto the helicopter.

00:22:10.080 --> 00:22:13.120
We flew to a military base about 50 miles away.

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:19.280
And then I got a call from the station and they said that George Tenet, the CIA director,

00:22:19.840 --> 00:22:27.360
had ordered, these were his exact words, 24-7 CIA eyes on, do not leave his bedside.

00:22:27.360 --> 00:22:29.920
And I ended up sitting there for the next 56 hours.

00:22:29.920 --> 00:22:34.080
I tore up his sheet. I tied him to the bed because I was afraid I was going to fall asleep.

00:22:34.800 --> 00:22:39.280
And I just sat there at the foot of the bed and stared at him for the next 56 hours.

00:22:39.280 --> 00:22:42.640
Wow. Okay, so then what happens? Where do you go next?

00:22:42.640 --> 00:22:48.240
Well, 24 hours into this, he starts to stir.

00:22:48.240 --> 00:22:52.800
And in the meantime, I had called one of the guys over at our safe house and I said,

00:22:52.800 --> 00:22:56.960
buddy, I smell so bad, I'm grossing myself out.

00:22:56.960 --> 00:22:59.760
I said, can you send over some clean clothes?

00:22:59.760 --> 00:23:02.960
The only clean clothes I had, I had a pair of underwear, a pair of socks,

00:23:02.960 --> 00:23:05.920
and a red t-shirt that my kids had bought me that I slept in.

00:23:06.800 --> 00:23:09.600
It had a SpongeBob SquarePants in the middle of it.

00:23:10.400 --> 00:23:14.080
So I put that on with my big poofy Pakistani balloon pants.

00:23:14.720 --> 00:23:19.920
And 24 hours later, he starts to stir. Remember, he's tied to the bed, so he's tied like this.

00:23:20.640 --> 00:23:27.600
And I stood up at the foot of the bed and he opened one eye and you could see the exact

00:23:27.600 --> 00:23:31.680
instant that he realized, oh my God, the Americans have me.

00:23:31.680 --> 00:23:39.040
Because he looked at SpongeBob and his heart rate went from 110 to 220 and he coded.

00:23:39.680 --> 00:23:45.280
And so they call the code blue, they rush into the room, they shock him with the paddles,

00:23:45.280 --> 00:23:49.600
they give him a shot of demoral, and then he's out again for another six hours.

00:23:50.160 --> 00:23:56.480
So six hours after that, he wakes up, he's terrified, and he motions for me to come next to him.

00:23:57.120 --> 00:24:01.920
So I move his oxygen mask off to the side and I said to him in Arabic,

00:24:01.920 --> 00:24:05.440
shuismek, what is your name? And he shakes his head.

00:24:05.440 --> 00:24:07.680
So I repeated it, shuismek.

00:24:07.760 --> 00:24:11.760
And he says to me in English, I will not speak to you in God's language.

00:24:12.640 --> 00:24:15.120
And I said, that's okay, Officer Vader, we know who you are.

00:24:15.120 --> 00:24:18.720
He starts crying and he says, please brother, kill me.

00:24:18.720 --> 00:24:20.560
Take the pillow and kill me.

00:24:20.560 --> 00:24:24.640
And I said, nobody's going to kill you, we've been looking for you for a long time.

00:24:24.640 --> 00:24:28.080
And I said, I don't know much, but I know that you're going to get the best

00:24:28.080 --> 00:24:30.560
medical care that the American government can provide.

00:24:31.840 --> 00:24:35.760
So he relaxed. Finally, he wanted to know what happened.

00:24:35.760 --> 00:24:37.360
He couldn't really remember what happened.

00:24:37.360 --> 00:24:39.200
I said, you tried to escape.

00:24:39.200 --> 00:24:44.240
He had climbed to the roof of his safe house to jump to the roof of the next door house.

00:24:44.240 --> 00:24:46.000
And the Pakistani guy shot him.

00:24:46.880 --> 00:24:49.760
And we talked a lot.

00:24:50.480 --> 00:24:53.280
We talked about Christianity versus Islam.

00:24:53.280 --> 00:24:54.800
He recited poetry.

00:24:54.800 --> 00:24:56.320
He cried a lot.

00:24:56.320 --> 00:24:58.720
He said he would never know the touch of a woman.

00:24:58.720 --> 00:25:01.120
He would never know the joy of fatherhood.

00:25:01.120 --> 00:25:03.920
And I said, listen, you're not the victim here.

00:25:03.920 --> 00:25:06.800
I said, there were 50,000 people in those towers.

00:25:06.800 --> 00:25:08.480
What did you think was going to happen?

00:25:08.480 --> 00:25:10.640
Did you think we wouldn't try to find you?

00:25:10.640 --> 00:25:11.600
To kill you?

00:25:11.600 --> 00:25:12.880
To kill Bin Laden?

00:25:12.880 --> 00:25:14.400
You're lucky you're alive.

00:25:14.400 --> 00:25:15.920
We could have killed you the other night.

00:25:16.800 --> 00:25:19.280
And he said, no, I only wanted to kill Jews.

00:25:19.280 --> 00:25:21.840
He said, I was overruled.

00:25:22.400 --> 00:25:25.520
And I said, well, be that as it may, you're not the victim here.

00:25:26.160 --> 00:25:30.080
And then finally I said to him, he was really worried about what was going to happen to him.

00:25:30.080 --> 00:25:33.040
And the honest to God's truth was I didn't know what was going to happen to him.

00:25:33.040 --> 00:25:34.160
I didn't have a need to know.

00:25:34.800 --> 00:25:38.080
And so I said to him, I'm being honest with you.

00:25:38.080 --> 00:25:41.600
I said, I don't know what's going to happen, but I'll tell you one thing.

00:25:42.160 --> 00:25:46.320
I am the nicest guy that you're going to meet in this experience.

00:25:46.960 --> 00:25:50.560
My colleagues go down hill after this.

00:25:50.560 --> 00:25:51.760
All down hill.

00:25:51.760 --> 00:25:56.560
I said, if I could give you one piece of advice, it's that you have to cooperate.

00:25:57.520 --> 00:26:02.960
And he said, you seem like a nice man, but you're the enemy and I'll never cooperate.

00:26:03.600 --> 00:26:04.880
I said, suit yourself.

00:26:05.600 --> 00:26:10.080
So a couple of hours after that, again, at about five in the morning,

00:26:10.080 --> 00:26:15.760
an unmarked CIA jet landed on the tarmac right outside the hospital room.

00:26:15.760 --> 00:26:18.160
The hospital was right on the tarmac on this base.

00:26:18.880 --> 00:26:21.280
And he asked me if I would hold his hand.

00:26:21.280 --> 00:26:22.800
He cried all the way to the plane.

00:26:22.800 --> 00:26:27.440
Three FBI agents and I carried his gurney out to the plane.

00:26:27.440 --> 00:26:29.920
It was hard to get him on because he was strapped to the gurney.

00:26:29.920 --> 00:26:32.560
So we sort of maneuvered him onto the plane.

00:26:32.560 --> 00:26:36.000
We strapped him on the luggage rack at the back.

00:26:36.800 --> 00:26:40.560
And I leaned over and I said, remember, you have to cooperate.

00:26:40.560 --> 00:26:43.600
And he squeezed my hand and I never saw him again.

00:26:44.240 --> 00:26:44.800
That was it.

00:26:45.360 --> 00:26:45.680
Yeah.

00:26:45.680 --> 00:26:46.400
That was it.

00:26:46.400 --> 00:26:49.920
You should have given him a BLT, bacon, lettuce, and tomato sandwich.

00:26:50.640 --> 00:26:52.880
What's the name of those pants that you said?

00:26:52.880 --> 00:26:54.400
The Salwar Commence.

00:26:54.400 --> 00:26:55.520
The Salwar Commence.

00:26:55.520 --> 00:26:56.640
That's the thing.

00:26:56.640 --> 00:26:57.440
I can't stop this.

00:26:57.440 --> 00:26:58.160
What was that food?

00:26:58.160 --> 00:26:58.880
MC Hammer.

00:26:58.880 --> 00:27:00.800
MC Hammer is the way to open that pants, right?

00:27:01.360 --> 00:27:02.000
Yeah.

00:27:02.000 --> 00:27:02.320
Okay.

00:27:02.320 --> 00:27:05.040
So now you don't see him again after this, right?

00:27:05.040 --> 00:27:06.080
No, I don't see him again.

00:27:06.080 --> 00:27:09.680
So when does this whole thing come out about the allegations against you?

00:27:10.400 --> 00:27:10.720
Yeah.

00:27:10.720 --> 00:27:13.600
Well, because of the strength of this capture,

00:27:13.600 --> 00:27:15.600
this was the senior most al-Qaeda person we had ever done.

00:27:15.600 --> 00:27:17.920
Well, wasn't the other guy under bin Laden,

00:27:17.920 --> 00:27:20.400
the guy that looks like a professor there with the glasses?

00:27:20.400 --> 00:27:20.960
What's his name?

00:27:20.960 --> 00:27:22.240
He's still at large.

00:27:22.240 --> 00:27:24.000
The al-Qaeda number two guy.

00:27:24.000 --> 00:27:24.400
Oh, yeah.

00:27:24.400 --> 00:27:25.440
Aiman Azawaki.

00:27:25.440 --> 00:27:26.240
The Azawaki.

00:27:26.240 --> 00:27:27.680
We never whacked him yet?

00:27:27.680 --> 00:27:28.080
No.

00:27:28.080 --> 00:27:29.200
Can't find the guy.

00:27:29.200 --> 00:27:30.160
Can't find it.

00:27:30.160 --> 00:27:31.200
Unbelievable.

00:27:31.200 --> 00:27:32.880
You know, and I wonder too,

00:27:32.880 --> 00:27:35.200
and I don't have any inside information here, Bo,

00:27:35.200 --> 00:27:38.160
but as an educated observer,

00:27:38.160 --> 00:27:40.400
I wonder if we're just not looking for him.

00:27:40.400 --> 00:27:43.120
Because al-Qaeda is pretty much irrelevant now.

00:27:43.120 --> 00:27:45.040
And I wonder if we just walked away.

00:27:45.040 --> 00:27:47.520
You know, we're focusing on ISIS and maybe other groups.

00:27:47.520 --> 00:27:49.600
We just don't care about al-Qaeda anymore.

00:27:49.600 --> 00:27:50.080
Yeah.

00:27:50.080 --> 00:27:51.520
If I saw him, I'd whack him.

00:27:51.520 --> 00:27:52.400
Yeah, I would, too.

00:27:52.400 --> 00:27:52.880
Okay.

00:27:52.880 --> 00:27:53.920
So now let's go.

00:27:53.920 --> 00:27:55.040
How do you get in trouble?

00:27:55.040 --> 00:27:56.720
Because as far as I'm concerned,

00:27:56.720 --> 00:27:58.160
you're a great American right now.

00:27:58.160 --> 00:27:58.960
What happened?

00:27:58.960 --> 00:27:59.440
Thank you.

00:27:59.440 --> 00:28:00.400
I'm a patriot.

00:28:00.400 --> 00:28:01.520
I want that to be clear.

00:28:01.520 --> 00:28:03.840
I'm an absolute patriot.

00:28:03.840 --> 00:28:05.840
So I go home to headquarters,

00:28:05.840 --> 00:28:08.000
and because of the capture, I got promoted.

00:28:08.000 --> 00:28:10.000
And they made me deputy assistant,

00:28:10.000 --> 00:28:11.680
I'm sorry, I was executive assistant

00:28:11.680 --> 00:28:14.720
to the deputy director of the CIA for operations.

00:28:14.720 --> 00:28:16.320
And in that position,

00:28:16.320 --> 00:28:18.240
you have access to literally everything

00:28:18.240 --> 00:28:19.440
around the world that's happening.

00:28:20.960 --> 00:28:22.320
And August 1st,

00:28:22.320 --> 00:28:25.120
well, before I even started in that job,

00:28:26.000 --> 00:28:27.120
I get back to headquarters,

00:28:27.120 --> 00:28:29.120
and I'm in the cafeteria one day.

00:28:29.120 --> 00:28:31.200
And a buddy of mine from the counterterrorism center

00:28:31.200 --> 00:28:32.160
comes up to me and he says,

00:28:32.160 --> 00:28:34.240
hey, I'm glad I ran into you.

00:28:34.240 --> 00:28:36.160
You want to be certified

00:28:36.160 --> 00:28:38.560
in the use of enhanced interrogation techniques?

00:28:38.560 --> 00:28:39.920
What are we talking about now?

00:28:40.480 --> 00:28:44.720
Yeah, this is a senior counterterrorism officer.

00:28:44.720 --> 00:28:45.120
What year?

00:28:45.920 --> 00:28:48.320
This is May of 2002.

00:28:48.320 --> 00:28:48.720
Go ahead.

00:28:49.520 --> 00:28:51.200
So I'd never heard the term

00:28:51.920 --> 00:28:53.680
enhanced interrogation techniques.

00:28:53.680 --> 00:28:54.560
I said, what is that?

00:28:55.280 --> 00:28:56.640
And so he explains it to me.

00:28:56.640 --> 00:28:57.600
And I said, man,

00:28:57.600 --> 00:28:59.360
that sounds like a torture program.

00:28:59.360 --> 00:29:00.160
Are you kidding me?

00:29:00.720 --> 00:29:02.800
And he said, no, no, it's not torture.

00:29:02.800 --> 00:29:04.320
The president signed off on it

00:29:04.320 --> 00:29:06.000
and DOJ said that we could do it.

00:29:06.640 --> 00:29:09.280
And I said, let me think about it for an hour.

00:29:09.280 --> 00:29:10.640
So I went up to the seventh floor.

00:29:10.640 --> 00:29:13.040
It's the executive floor of the CIA.

00:29:13.040 --> 00:29:15.680
There was a very, very senior CIA officer

00:29:15.680 --> 00:29:17.120
up there who I had worked for

00:29:17.120 --> 00:29:18.480
about a decade earlier.

00:29:18.480 --> 00:29:19.680
So I knocked on his door and I said,

00:29:19.680 --> 00:29:21.200
hey, I need some advice.

00:29:21.200 --> 00:29:22.800
They just asked me if I want to do these

00:29:22.800 --> 00:29:24.560
enhanced interrogation techniques.

00:29:25.440 --> 00:29:27.120
And he says to me, listen,

00:29:27.840 --> 00:29:29.920
first of all, let's call the spade a spade.

00:29:29.920 --> 00:29:31.680
This is a torture program.

00:29:31.680 --> 00:29:33.360
They can call it whatever they want.

00:29:33.360 --> 00:29:35.680
They can use whatever euphemism they want.

00:29:35.680 --> 00:29:37.360
This is a torture program.

00:29:37.360 --> 00:29:39.040
And torture is a slippery slope.

00:29:39.040 --> 00:29:41.120
And you know how these guys are, he says.

00:29:41.120 --> 00:29:42.720
Somebody's going to go overboard

00:29:42.720 --> 00:29:44.000
and they're going to kill a prisoner.

00:29:44.800 --> 00:29:45.680
And when that happens,

00:29:45.680 --> 00:29:47.680
there's going to be a congressional investigation.

00:29:47.680 --> 00:29:49.520
Then there's going to be a justice department

00:29:49.520 --> 00:29:51.760
investigation and somebody's going to go to prison.

00:29:51.760 --> 00:29:53.200
Do you want to go to prison?

00:29:53.200 --> 00:29:55.120
And I said, no, I don't want to go to prison.

00:29:55.120 --> 00:29:56.880
It turned out I was the only person

00:29:56.880 --> 00:29:57.840
who went to prison.

00:29:57.840 --> 00:29:59.840
But I went back downstairs and went back.

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:04.960
to this senior officer and I said, this is a torture program. I'm not interested in it.

00:30:05.520 --> 00:30:11.520
And so I went about my business. Well, August 1st, 2002, we start torturing Abu Zubeda.

00:30:11.520 --> 00:30:13.280
Oh, you're a friend. You start torturing him.

00:30:13.840 --> 00:30:14.480
Yeah.

00:30:14.480 --> 00:30:17.200
Oh, and then you saw him again. I thought you didn't see him.

00:30:17.200 --> 00:30:22.240
No, no, no. I was in headquarters. They were torturing him at a secret prison overseas.

00:30:22.240 --> 00:30:22.320
Yeah.

00:30:22.320 --> 00:30:25.360
But I'm reading the cable traffic as it's coming back.

00:30:25.360 --> 00:30:27.520
Were you there at the scene of the torture?

00:30:27.520 --> 00:30:28.880
No, I was not.

00:30:28.880 --> 00:30:34.720
Oh, so you know that because you were high up, you knew they were interrogating him. Good.

00:30:34.720 --> 00:30:38.400
And I had to brief the director every morning about all this stuff that was going down.

00:30:38.400 --> 00:30:39.920
And who was the director at that time?

00:30:40.560 --> 00:30:42.160
Yes, George Tennant.

00:30:42.160 --> 00:30:42.800
Okay, good.

00:30:43.760 --> 00:30:52.960
So I said, this is a war crime. I mean, we're signatories to the United Nations Convention

00:30:53.040 --> 00:30:58.480
Against Torture. And we have the Federal Torture Act of 1946.

00:30:58.480 --> 00:31:01.520
It specifically outlaws exactly these techniques.

00:31:01.520 --> 00:31:04.720
And to tell you the truth, I waited for somebody to go public.

00:31:04.720 --> 00:31:09.600
I just figured somebody would because as we're torturing him, people are,

00:31:10.800 --> 00:31:13.120
they're objecting in cable traffic.

00:31:13.120 --> 00:31:18.160
And some of them actually returned to headquarters from overseas because they had such serious

00:31:18.240 --> 00:31:20.880
objections. But nobody said anything.

00:31:22.400 --> 00:31:27.440
So two years later, I resigned from the CIA to go into the private sector.

00:31:27.440 --> 00:31:28.480
I've got five kids.

00:31:28.480 --> 00:31:30.400
I needed to make some money, put them through college.

00:31:31.200 --> 00:31:34.480
And still I kept waiting for somebody to say something.

00:31:34.480 --> 00:31:35.520
Now, what year is this now?

00:31:36.480 --> 00:31:39.600
This was 2004.

00:31:39.600 --> 00:31:40.640
I left the CIA.

00:31:40.640 --> 00:31:41.680
Good. You left the CIA.

00:31:41.680 --> 00:31:42.880
You went to private practice.

00:31:42.880 --> 00:31:43.200
Good.

00:31:43.200 --> 00:31:44.080
Yeah. 2004.

00:31:44.080 --> 00:31:45.520
I went into the private sector.

00:31:46.160 --> 00:31:50.080
And then in December of 2007, and this is where the shit hit the fan,

00:31:50.640 --> 00:31:55.520
December of 2007, I get a call from Brian Ross of ABC News.

00:31:55.520 --> 00:31:58.480
Now, I've never spoken to a reporter before.

00:31:59.200 --> 00:32:04.800
So Brian Ross tells me that he has a source who said that I had tortured Abu Zubaydah.

00:32:04.800 --> 00:32:07.040
I said, absolutely untrue.

00:32:07.040 --> 00:32:08.800
I was kind to Abu Zubaydah.

00:32:08.800 --> 00:32:10.720
I was the only person who was.

00:32:10.720 --> 00:32:12.720
I said, I've never tortured anybody.

00:32:12.720 --> 00:32:15.360
I never laid a hand on Abu Zubaydah or anybody else.

00:32:15.440 --> 00:32:18.720
I said, your source is either mistaken or he's a liar.

00:32:19.520 --> 00:32:24.000
And Ross says to me, and I had no idea this was an old reporter's trick.

00:32:24.000 --> 00:32:26.160
He said, you're welcome to come on the show and defend yourself.

00:32:27.120 --> 00:32:28.320
I said, I'll think about it.

00:32:29.200 --> 00:32:33.520
In the meantime, a couple of days later, President Bush gives a press conference.

00:32:34.400 --> 00:32:38.960
And he looks right into the camera boat, looks right into the camera and he says,

00:32:38.960 --> 00:32:40.800
we do not torture.

00:32:41.680 --> 00:32:44.480
Well, I was sitting with my wife, now my ex-wife,

00:32:44.480 --> 00:32:46.880
but she was also a senior CIA officer.

00:32:46.880 --> 00:32:50.000
And I said, he is a bald-faced liar.

00:32:50.000 --> 00:32:52.720
He is looking the American people in the eye and he's lying.

00:32:53.840 --> 00:32:57.840
And then a couple of days later, he's walking from the back of the White House

00:32:57.840 --> 00:33:00.800
onto the helicopter to go to Camp David.

00:33:00.800 --> 00:33:04.240
And a reporter shouts a question and Bush turns around and he says,

00:33:04.240 --> 00:33:08.480
well, if there is torture, it's the result of a rogue CIA officer.

00:33:09.360 --> 00:33:12.560
And I said to my wife, I said,

00:33:13.040 --> 00:33:17.120
So they knew that you were talking with the ABC guy.

00:33:17.120 --> 00:33:18.000
Go ahead.

00:33:18.000 --> 00:33:21.280
But I hadn't even said anything.

00:33:21.840 --> 00:33:25.760
But I said, Brian Ross's source is at the White House and they're going to pin this on me.

00:33:26.400 --> 00:33:30.560
So I decided that I was going to do Brian Ross's interview

00:33:30.560 --> 00:33:32.160
and I was going to tell the truth.

00:33:32.160 --> 00:33:34.240
This was an illegal program.

00:33:34.240 --> 00:33:35.760
It was unconstitutional.

00:33:36.560 --> 00:33:38.560
And I believed it was a war crime.

00:33:38.560 --> 00:33:42.880
It was anti-American to carry out a program like this.

00:33:43.600 --> 00:33:48.320
You know, and this is why the FBI, when we started torturing Abu Zubaydah,

00:33:48.320 --> 00:33:54.240
literally every FBI agent in the country, in that foreign country where we were torturing him,

00:33:54.240 --> 00:33:55.440
left the country.

00:33:55.440 --> 00:33:58.560
Because not only did they want no part of a torture program,

00:33:58.560 --> 00:34:02.880
they didn't even want to be in the same country where the torture was taken place.

00:34:03.760 --> 00:34:05.840
So I went public December of 07.

00:34:06.000 --> 00:34:06.960
Yeah.

00:34:06.960 --> 00:34:08.400
Here's where it gets weird, Bo.

00:34:09.680 --> 00:34:15.120
The FBI investigated me from December of 07 to December of 08.

00:34:15.120 --> 00:34:19.120
And in December of 08, they determined that I had not committed a crime

00:34:19.120 --> 00:34:20.160
and they closed the case.

00:34:20.160 --> 00:34:23.920
They even sent my attorneys a declination letter declining to prosecute me.

00:34:25.360 --> 00:34:30.880
I had no idea that three weeks later, when Barack Obama became president,

00:34:31.760 --> 00:34:37.600
that John Brennan asked the Justice Department to secretly reopen the case against me.

00:34:37.600 --> 00:34:41.280
I had no idea that my phones were being monitored,

00:34:41.280 --> 00:34:43.680
that my emails were being intercepted.

00:34:43.680 --> 00:34:44.640
There was surveillance.

00:34:44.640 --> 00:34:47.200
Another Pfizer warrant.

00:34:47.200 --> 00:34:47.600
Here we go.

00:34:47.600 --> 00:34:49.120
Yeah. Exactly.

00:34:49.920 --> 00:34:53.520
Now, the thing is, under the whistleblower protection,

00:34:53.520 --> 00:34:55.520
why weren't you protected under that?

00:34:56.880 --> 00:34:58.960
That is the best question.

00:34:58.960 --> 00:35:04.640
It's because national security whistleblowers are exempt from the protections of the

00:35:04.640 --> 00:35:06.320
Whistleblower Protection Act.

00:35:06.320 --> 00:35:08.000
Yeah, but right now we have a whistleblower.

00:35:08.000 --> 00:35:10.400
It's supposed to be involved with national security.

00:35:10.400 --> 00:35:12.720
Security ratting out the Trump.

00:35:12.720 --> 00:35:13.440
Ratting out Trump.

00:35:14.480 --> 00:35:14.720
Yeah.

00:35:15.760 --> 00:35:19.360
They amended the law in 2015 after I got out of prison.

00:35:19.360 --> 00:35:19.920
Ah.

00:35:19.920 --> 00:35:21.360
And so now you're protected.

00:35:22.240 --> 00:35:23.600
Although let me say for the record.

00:35:23.600 --> 00:35:24.880
Let's back up again.

00:35:24.880 --> 00:35:25.920
2012.

00:35:25.920 --> 00:35:26.560
Let's go back.

00:35:26.560 --> 00:35:30.240
So now all of a sudden, Brenny comes in with Obama in 08.

00:35:30.240 --> 00:35:32.800
Now they reopen the case in what, in 09?

00:35:33.360 --> 00:35:34.000
Yeah.

00:35:34.000 --> 00:35:36.320
In 09, January of 09.

00:35:36.320 --> 00:35:38.960
And when do you get the next call from that you have a problem?

00:35:40.240 --> 00:35:42.400
In January of 12.

00:35:42.400 --> 00:35:43.760
That many years later?

00:35:44.320 --> 00:35:44.880
Yeah.

00:35:44.880 --> 00:35:45.840
So check this out.

00:35:46.560 --> 00:35:51.760
In the interim, I start working for John Kerry on the Senate Foreign Relations Committee.

00:35:51.760 --> 00:35:52.800
Good friend of mine, John.

00:35:53.360 --> 00:35:54.880
He was a decent man.

00:35:54.880 --> 00:35:56.960
He used to hang out at Mar-a-Lago.

00:35:56.960 --> 00:35:59.280
Me and John Kerry, when his wife was alive,

00:35:59.280 --> 00:36:02.800
she couldn't handle the booze that well, but John's a good guy.

00:36:02.800 --> 00:36:03.440
I like him.

00:36:03.440 --> 00:36:06.000
We don't agree on a lot of things, but John's a good guy.

00:36:07.040 --> 00:36:10.800
Well, I'm working for him as the chief investigator on the Senate Foreign Relations

00:36:10.800 --> 00:36:11.040
Committee.

00:36:11.040 --> 00:36:14.400
And one of the great things about that job is you get to have lunch with foreign

00:36:14.400 --> 00:36:15.600
diplomats all the time.

00:36:15.600 --> 00:36:17.600
It's a great exchange of information.

00:36:17.600 --> 00:36:22.240
So one day in 2011, remember, I have no idea I'm under investigation.

00:36:22.320 --> 00:36:27.760
One day in 2011, I get a call from a Japanese diplomat and he wants to have lunch.

00:36:27.760 --> 00:36:29.840
So we have lunch, lovely lunch.

00:36:29.840 --> 00:36:33.600
We talked about, I remember it well, we talked about Turkish elections,

00:36:33.600 --> 00:36:36.160
Israeli elections, the Middle East peace process.

00:36:36.160 --> 00:36:39.040
And at the end of the lunch, he says to me, so what's next for you?

00:36:39.600 --> 00:36:41.920
And I said, actually, I think I'm going to resign soon.

00:36:41.920 --> 00:36:44.160
I promised Senator Kerry I'd give him two years.

00:36:44.160 --> 00:36:45.520
It's been two and a half.

00:36:45.520 --> 00:36:46.800
I got a bunch of kids at home.

00:36:46.800 --> 00:36:47.760
I got to put through college.

00:36:47.760 --> 00:36:49.520
I'm going to go back into the private sector.

00:36:50.160 --> 00:36:53.040
And he goes, he whispers, he goes, no, don't do that.

00:36:53.600 --> 00:36:57.280
If you give me information, I can give you money.

00:36:58.720 --> 00:37:00.960
And I said, what the hell's wrong with you?

00:37:01.520 --> 00:37:04.800
Do you have any idea how many times I've made that pitch in my career?

00:37:04.800 --> 00:37:06.720
You're just going to cold pitch me?

00:37:06.720 --> 00:37:08.400
Shame on you, I said.

00:37:08.400 --> 00:37:12.960
And I went and I mean, without stopping, I went literally directly

00:37:13.680 --> 00:37:15.840
to the office of the Senate security officer.

00:37:15.840 --> 00:37:18.880
And I said, I was just pitched by a foreign intelligence officer.

00:37:18.880 --> 00:37:20.560
What you have to do, right?

00:37:20.560 --> 00:37:21.360
You have to do.

00:37:21.360 --> 00:37:22.880
You're compelled by law.

00:37:22.880 --> 00:37:23.600
Yeah.

00:37:23.600 --> 00:37:23.840
Yes.

00:37:24.400 --> 00:37:26.160
So I reported it immediately.

00:37:26.960 --> 00:37:30.720
And he told me to write it up and he called the FBI.

00:37:30.720 --> 00:37:34.000
Day later, two FBI agents come to see me at my office

00:37:34.000 --> 00:37:37.120
and they asked me to tell them the story again.

00:37:37.120 --> 00:37:38.000
So I did.

00:37:38.000 --> 00:37:39.600
And they said, here's what we want you to do.

00:37:39.600 --> 00:37:43.760
We want you to call him back, invite him to lunch,

00:37:44.320 --> 00:37:47.760
try to get him to tell you exactly what information he wants

00:37:47.760 --> 00:37:49.520
and what he's willing to pay for it.

00:37:49.520 --> 00:37:49.840
Right.

00:37:50.560 --> 00:37:51.520
I said, okay.

00:37:52.160 --> 00:37:53.120
So I did.

00:37:53.840 --> 00:37:56.880
And I wrote them another detailed memo.

00:37:56.880 --> 00:37:59.920
They asked me to do it a third time and a fourth time.

00:37:59.920 --> 00:38:01.360
How much money was involved?

00:38:02.160 --> 00:38:02.640
Zero.

00:38:02.640 --> 00:38:04.160
Oh, you with with him?

00:38:04.160 --> 00:38:04.880
Yeah.

00:38:04.880 --> 00:38:05.120
Yeah.

00:38:05.120 --> 00:38:07.840
He was offering me $5,000 a month.

00:38:07.840 --> 00:38:08.400
That's it?

00:38:09.520 --> 00:38:12.080
Believe it or not, that's kind of standard pay for a

00:38:12.800 --> 00:38:17.760
for a sort of run of the mill spy.

00:38:18.640 --> 00:38:19.200
Oh, really?

00:38:19.200 --> 00:38:20.160
So, yeah.

00:38:21.440 --> 00:38:23.040
That's bog of a basement to me.

00:38:23.440 --> 00:38:24.160
Yeah.

00:38:24.160 --> 00:38:28.480
I'm reporting all of this in excruciating detail back to the FBI.

00:38:28.480 --> 00:38:29.200
Okay.

00:38:29.200 --> 00:38:33.040
In the final lunch, he says he got promoted.

00:38:33.040 --> 00:38:35.600
He's being transferred to his dream job.

00:38:35.600 --> 00:38:39.280
He's going to be the number two at the Japanese Embassy in Cairo.

00:38:39.280 --> 00:38:40.480
I shake his hand.

00:38:41.040 --> 00:38:42.640
And I never saw him again.

00:38:42.640 --> 00:38:45.920
A year later, December of 12th, I get arrested.

00:38:46.560 --> 00:38:49.520
I'm charged with three counts of espionage,

00:38:50.080 --> 00:38:52.000
one count of making a false statement.

00:38:52.000 --> 00:38:54.080
And we were never really sure what the false statement was

00:38:54.080 --> 00:38:56.960
supposed to have been and one count of violating the

00:38:56.960 --> 00:38:59.600
Intelligence Identities Act of 1981.

00:39:00.560 --> 00:39:04.080
So we get discovery from the Justice Department,

00:39:04.080 --> 00:39:07.440
15,000 pages of classified information.

00:39:07.440 --> 00:39:11.680
And in this discovery, we get a series of memos.

00:39:12.880 --> 00:39:17.760
First of all, there's a memo from John Brennan to Eric Holder

00:39:17.760 --> 00:39:19.600
saying, charge him with espionage.

00:39:20.400 --> 00:39:22.560
And Holder responds,

00:39:22.560 --> 00:39:24.880
my people don't think he committed espionage.

00:39:25.680 --> 00:39:28.320
And then Brennan writes back and says,

00:39:28.320 --> 00:39:30.480
charge him anyway and make him defend himself.

00:39:31.520 --> 00:39:35.840
But then we get these memos that show that there never was

00:39:35.920 --> 00:39:37.280
any Japanese diplomat.

00:39:37.840 --> 00:39:39.760
He was an FBI agent.

00:39:39.760 --> 00:39:41.200
And they were setting you up.

00:39:41.200 --> 00:39:42.800
And they were setting me up.

00:39:42.800 --> 00:39:43.200
Why?

00:39:43.200 --> 00:39:44.160
Did you take the 5,000?

00:39:44.800 --> 00:39:46.800
I didn't take one red cent.

00:39:46.800 --> 00:39:48.160
And I paid for the lunches.

00:39:49.600 --> 00:39:53.360
And what they ended up doing was the Japanese

00:39:53.360 --> 00:39:56.480
American FBI agent sent a memo to his boss,

00:39:56.480 --> 00:39:59.840
Peter Strock, saying he's not going to take the

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:02.800
He keeps reporting the contact.

00:40:02.800 --> 00:40:03.800
Let's just end this.

00:40:03.800 --> 00:40:04.800
Yeah.

00:40:04.800 --> 00:40:07.640
And so that's why they came up with the story that he had gotten transferred.

00:40:07.640 --> 00:40:12.160
But they tried repeatedly to set me up on an espionage charge, and I kept reporting it

00:40:12.160 --> 00:40:13.960
back to the FBI.

00:40:13.960 --> 00:40:19.320
And so all the espionage charges were finally dropped, but what they do is something called

00:40:19.320 --> 00:40:20.320
charge stacking.

00:40:20.320 --> 00:40:22.600
They'll charge you with a whole bunch of felons.

00:40:22.600 --> 00:40:24.000
And they beat you into the ground.

00:40:24.000 --> 00:40:25.000
Yeah.

00:40:25.000 --> 00:40:26.000
They wait until you go bankrupt.

00:40:26.000 --> 00:40:28.160
And then they come back and they say, all right, we'll dismiss all the charges

00:40:28.160 --> 00:40:31.680
but one, if you take a plea, what are you going to do?

00:40:31.680 --> 00:40:34.200
You didn't go to trial where this could all be exposed.

00:40:34.200 --> 00:40:37.560
You couldn't afford it, right, John?

00:40:37.560 --> 00:40:41.800
I spent $1.15 million on attorneys.

00:40:41.800 --> 00:40:47.880
I still owe them $880,000 that they'll never see.

00:40:47.880 --> 00:40:52.240
And I ended up, instead of 45 years, I ended up with 23 months.

00:40:52.240 --> 00:40:53.240
Wow.

00:40:53.240 --> 00:40:54.240
Yeah.

00:40:54.240 --> 00:40:55.240
Yeah, five kids.

00:40:55.240 --> 00:40:58.120
I mean, you know, I mean, just the hittiest thing.

00:40:58.120 --> 00:41:01.000
These stories, you just can't believe it.

00:41:01.000 --> 00:41:04.960
But if you would have went to trial and got convicted, you could have gone away for a long

00:41:04.960 --> 00:41:05.960
time, right?

00:41:05.960 --> 00:41:10.960
Well, I asked my attorneys because I turned down every deal until the very end.

00:41:10.960 --> 00:41:14.680
And even the 23 months, I initially turned it down because I kept saying, as soon

00:41:14.680 --> 00:41:18.000
as I get in front of a jury, they're going to realize how ridiculous this is.

00:41:18.000 --> 00:41:19.200
I'm innocent.

00:41:19.200 --> 00:41:22.680
This is a setup, and I'm going to be acquitted.

00:41:22.680 --> 00:41:30.680
So my boss, my boss, my lead attorney, pulled me aside, and he said, he was angry.

00:41:30.680 --> 00:41:32.680
And he said, you know what your problem is?

00:41:32.680 --> 00:41:36.880
Your problem is you think this is about justice, and it's not about justice.

00:41:36.880 --> 00:41:38.960
It's about mitigating damage.

00:41:38.960 --> 00:41:40.880
Take the deal.

00:41:40.880 --> 00:41:47.560
And so I took the deal, and I said to him, if I turn the deal down and I go to trial

00:41:47.560 --> 00:41:51.640
and I lose, what am I really looking at here?

00:41:51.640 --> 00:41:55.920
And he said, you're looking at realistically 12 to 18 years.

00:41:55.920 --> 00:41:58.360
Take the fucking deal, he said.

00:41:58.360 --> 00:41:59.360
So I took the deal.

00:41:59.360 --> 00:42:03.200
What do you think the intelligence community came after you?

00:42:03.200 --> 00:42:07.040
Oh, they were furious that I had aired the dirty laundry.

00:42:07.040 --> 00:42:13.440
You know, there are these internal systems in place where if you don't like something,

00:42:13.440 --> 00:42:15.440
you can complain.

00:42:15.440 --> 00:42:18.800
But my chain of command created the CIA's torture program.

00:42:18.800 --> 00:42:21.800
They implemented the CIA's torture program.

00:42:21.800 --> 00:42:24.880
So they were furious that I had gone public.

00:42:24.880 --> 00:42:25.880
It was unforgivable.

00:42:25.880 --> 00:42:31.560
In fact, that was a word that a former colleague used with me, that what I had done was unforgivable.

00:42:31.560 --> 00:42:32.560
I went public.

00:42:32.560 --> 00:42:33.560
Yeah.

00:42:33.560 --> 00:42:36.360
And what we're starting to see with Brennan, what's the other guy's name?

00:42:36.360 --> 00:42:37.360
Yeah.

00:42:37.360 --> 00:42:41.400
National Security Advisor there, Brennan, and what's the other guy, the other clown

00:42:41.400 --> 00:42:42.400
from CIA?

00:42:42.400 --> 00:42:45.360
And then he's on CNN all the time.

00:42:45.480 --> 00:42:52.560
Oh, not National Security Advisor, DNI, Clapper.

00:42:52.560 --> 00:42:58.680
Clapper, yeah, Clapper and his other jerk, or Brennan, and they're exposing everything.

00:42:58.680 --> 00:43:03.800
They were the leaks on it, whether you like Donald Trump or not, they've been leaking

00:43:03.800 --> 00:43:10.520
like son of a bitches, and they were the head of the intelligence organization.

00:43:10.520 --> 00:43:13.000
And they've been leaking stuff right on TV.

00:43:13.000 --> 00:43:14.000
Yeah.

00:43:14.000 --> 00:43:15.000
That's right.

00:43:15.000 --> 00:43:16.600
They're leakers, and they're both liars.

00:43:16.600 --> 00:43:22.840
I mean, we all saw the footage of Clapper lying under oath to the Senate Intelligence

00:43:22.840 --> 00:43:27.120
Committee saying that NSA doesn't intercept the communications of American citizens.

00:43:27.120 --> 00:43:28.120
Yeah.

00:43:28.120 --> 00:43:30.280
They lie, they leak, and they get away with it.

00:43:30.280 --> 00:43:32.520
John, so what are you doing now, John?

00:43:32.520 --> 00:43:40.200
Well, I've got a radio show here in Washington every day from 4 to 6, the afternoon drive.

00:43:40.200 --> 00:43:43.840
And I write a weekly syndicated newspaper column.

00:43:43.840 --> 00:43:44.840
I write books.

00:43:44.840 --> 00:43:45.840
I give speeches.

00:43:45.840 --> 00:43:48.400
You know, little here, little there, I patch together a living.

00:43:48.400 --> 00:43:51.560
Was he friends with our friend from the Swiss bank?

00:43:51.560 --> 00:43:52.560
Yes.

00:43:52.560 --> 00:43:53.560
Yeah, Brad Birkenfeld.

00:43:53.560 --> 00:43:55.200
I think that's how we get in touch.

00:43:55.200 --> 00:43:57.960
In fact, I'm having dinner with Brad tonight.

00:43:57.960 --> 00:43:58.960
Brad is great here.

00:43:58.960 --> 00:44:00.080
Well, Brad, that'll be with me tomorrow.

00:44:00.080 --> 00:44:05.320
And he has some interesting story, but that has like a kind of a similar thing.

00:44:05.320 --> 00:44:09.240
This guy was opening up, and nothing ever was done with all these son of a bitches

00:44:09.240 --> 00:44:12.560
not paying taxes, and it pisses me off.

00:44:12.560 --> 00:44:13.560
And you know what?

00:44:13.560 --> 00:44:16.520
They should have went after them and all these high-falutin' people with these accounts.

00:44:16.520 --> 00:44:18.020
It's unbelievable.

00:44:18.020 --> 00:44:19.020
But you know what?

00:44:19.020 --> 00:44:22.640
I just, I, you know, we got to wrap it up because we're all time-constrained here.

00:44:22.640 --> 00:44:24.480
But let me tell you something, John.

00:44:24.480 --> 00:44:26.600
You're quite an American patriot.

00:44:26.600 --> 00:44:30.320
And guys like you, whether you agree with the water board or not, I have my own personal

00:44:30.320 --> 00:44:35.480
opinions about water board, and I have my own, my personal opinions about torture.

00:44:35.480 --> 00:44:40.520
And when, you know, I was down in 9-11, I'll never forget what the sight I saw.

00:44:40.520 --> 00:44:46.920
I'll never forget what I observed with these terrorists and my own feelings on something.

00:44:46.920 --> 00:44:51.760
If I had someone that was going to explode a nuclear bomb in New York City, I may have

00:44:51.760 --> 00:44:52.760
done it a different way.

